</Field Guns Equipment>

<overview>
<p>Jump infantry platoons equip their troops with personal jump packs that allow short powered hops across broken terrain, over walls, and onto rooftops. This vertical mobility gives jump platoons access to positions that foot and motorized infantry cannot reach, making them particularly effective in urban combat, mountainous terrain, and defensive operations where elevation matters. The trade-off is cost and logistics: jump packs require fuel, maintenance, and trained operators, limiting the number of jump platoons a garrison command can field compared to standard foot formations.</p><p>Heavy weapons teams carry recoilless rifles that provide a portable anti-armor capability, allowing infantry to engage vehicles and hardened positions that would otherwise require dedicated support weapons. Eventually retired in favor of infantry-scale SRMs these weapons later experienced a resurgence as budgets and access to complex manufacturing were threatened. A rare few are equipped with heavier weapons and armor than is commonly found, allowing these teams to assault well protected objectives and hold critical positions as long as possible.</p>
</overview>

<notes>
Intro date based on introduction date of ballistic plate armor.
</notes>

